prompts.prompt:
Design a simple money model for my business. One of each, in order, and only things I can deliver.

My main offer and price: {{main_offer}}
My buyer: {{buyer}}
My costs per sale: {{costs}}
Hours I can deliver per week: {{capacity}}

Build:
1. Way in: a free or low-price first step that gets a buyer to try me.
2. Main offer: tighten what I have.
3. Upgrade: something bigger for buyers who want more. When to offer it (the moment they'd want it).
4. Smaller option: for people who say no to the main offer. What I remove to make it cheaper.
5. Monthly plan: what repeats every month so revenue continues.

For each: what it is, price, what it costs me, the margin, and the exact moment I offer it.

Then show the math in a table: if I get 10 buyers into the way in each month, with your best-guess conversion at each step, what lands each month. Label every conversion rate as an assumption I should test. Flag any step that eats too much of my time for my capacity.

Help me price this. Show the math.

What I'm selling: {{service}}
Who buys it: {{buyer}}
Time it takes me: {{time}}
My costs (tools, materials, travel): {{costs}}
What competitors charge, if known: {{competitors}}

Work out:
1. My floor: the lowest price where this is still worth my time. Use an hourly target of {{hourly_target}} plus costs.
2. The buyer's value: what this is worth to them (a new customer's value to their business, hours saved, money saved). Show the assumptions.
3. The market range from competitors.
4. A recommended price with a one-line reason, and a "good, better, best" set of three options.
5. How to say the price: frame the cost as the smallest true number (per week, per video, per day), and the value as the biggest true number.
6. What to say when they say "too expensive", three ways, none of them a discount.

Label every assumption. Don't invent competitor prices; say "unknown" if you can't find them.

Write a sales page for this offer. A stranger must understand what it is, who it's for and what to do next within five seconds of landing.

Offer: {{offer}}
Buyer: {{buyer}}
Proof I can show (only real things): {{proof}}
Call to action: {{cta}}
Voice: {{voice}}

Sections, in order:
1. Hero: headline (under 10 words, concrete outcome), subhead (who it's for and how), one button.
2. The problem, in the buyer's words.
3. What you get: 3 to 5 deliverables, each a concrete thing.
4. How it works: 3 steps.
5. Proof: only what I gave you. If I gave nothing, use a demonstration (show the work) instead of testimonials.
6. Objections answered as a short FAQ (price, time, "will this work for me", how to cancel).
7. The offer stack and price, with the risk reversal I can honor.
8. Final call to action.

Rules: no hype words (revolutionary, game-changing, unlock), no invented reviews, numbers or logos, no promises of results the buyer gets. Short sentences. After the page, list the three lines you think are weakest and rewrite them.

Help me build my first 25: the 25 people or businesses I should contact first.

What I sell: {{offer}}
Who buys it: {{buyer}}
Where: {{where}}

Step 1. Tell me exactly where to find them (search terms, directories, platforms, hashtags, communities) and what to look for so I can build the list myself from their own websites and profiles. Don't copy listings out of Google Maps.
Step 2. Give me the columns for my list: name, website or profile, contact route, the gap I noticed, how bad the gap is (1 to 5), and why they'd care.
Step 3. Give me the gaps to check for each one, specific to my offer (for example: no website, no booking link, last post 3 months ago, no creator content in their ads, weak product photos).
Step 4. Give me a scoring rule to rank the 25 so I start with the most likely buyers.
Step 5. For the top 3, tell me the one free sample or mock-up I could make that shows them the fix.

If I paste my list back in, rank it and write a first line for each one that mentions the specific gap I found.

Write my first message to this prospect. Short, specific to them, and it gives before it asks.

Who: {{prospect}}
What I noticed about them: {{gap}}
What I made for them (sample, mock-up, audit): {{sample}}
What I sell: {{offer}}
Channel: {{channel}}
My name and business: {{me}}

Rules:
- Under 80 words for a DM, under 120 for email.
- First line is about them, not me. Mention the specific thing I noticed.
- Point to the sample I made. No attachment walls, no "hop on a call" in the first message.
- One easy question to reply to.
- No flattery that sounds automated, no fake urgency, no promise of results.
- If it's an email: include my business name, a mailing address line placeholder [ADDRESS], and a one-line way to opt out ("Not interested? Reply no and I won't follow up").

Write three versions: plain, warm, and bold. Then tell me which you'd send and why.

Write a follow-up sequence for prospects who didn't reply to my first message.

First message I sent: {{first_message}}
What I sell: {{offer}}
Channel: {{channel}}

Write 4 follow-ups:
- Day 3: a short bump that adds one new useful thing (a tip, a second idea, a result from the sample).
- Day 7: a different angle (a question about their goal, or a quick before and after).
- Day 14: social proof or a demonstration, only if I have it: {{proof}}
- Day 21: a polite close-the-loop message that makes it easy to say "not now".

Rules: each under 60 words, no guilt ("just bumping this", "did you see my last message"), each one stands alone. Respect a "no": if they say no or stop, they come off the list. Give me a reminder schedule I can paste into my calendar or tracker.

Write a pitch to a brand for paid content.

Brand: {{brand}}
What I noticed about their content or ads: {{gap}}
My niche and audience: {{me}}
What I'd make for them: {{deliverable}}
Sample I can show: {{sample}}

Write:
1. An email (under 120 words) and a DM (under 60 words). First line is about their brand. Point to my sample. One clear ask.
2. Three content ideas specific to their product, each with a hook line.
3. A simple rate card: per video, a 3-pack, and usage rights options. Mark these as starting points I should adjust.
4. A follow-up for day 5.

Rules: no inflated audience numbers, no claims about sales I'll drive. Include my business name and an opt-out line in the email.

Write 30 hooks for short videos about my business. A hook is the first line said or shown in the first 2 seconds.

My business: {{business}}
Who I want watching: {{audience}}
What they struggle with: {{pain}}
Proof or story I can use (only true things): {{story}}

Give 5 hooks in each of these styles:
1. Call out the viewer ("If you own a gym and...").
2. A surprising number or fact (true and sourced, or from my own work).
3. Before and after.
4. A mistake people make.
5. "I tried X so you don't have to" or a demonstration.
6. A strong opinion they'll want to argue with.

Rules: under 12 words each, plain language, no clickbait I can't pay off in the video, no income claims, no "you won't believe". Mark your top 5 and say what the video must show in the first 5 seconds to pay each one off.

Plan 30 days of content for my business.

Business: {{business}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Platforms: {{platforms}}
Posts per week I can actually make: {{frequency}}
What I sell and the free thing I give away: {{offers}}

Build:
1. Three content pillars with one line each (for example: show the work, teach one thing, behind the scenes).
2. A calendar table: date, platform, pillar, hook, format (talking head, screen recording, before and after, carousel), what to film or design, call to action.
3. Mix: about 70% useful or entertaining, 20% proof and process, 10% direct offers.
4. One weekly "repeat what worked" slot: remake last week's best post in a new way.
5. A batch plan: how to film or make the whole week in one session.

Rules: realistic for one person, no trends that need a big budget, every CTA points to my free thing or a DM keyword.

Write a one-page brand guide for my business so everything I make looks and sounds consistent.

Business: {{business}}
Who it's for: {{audience}}
Three words for how it should feel: {{feel}}
Brands I admire and why: {{references}}
Examples of my writing: {{samples}}

Include:
1. Positioning in one sentence.
2. Voice: we are / we are not table, writing rules, words to use and avoid.
3. Three taglines to choose from.
4. Visual direction: a color palette (hex codes), two fonts (free ones), photo style, and what to never do.
5. Templates to make once: post, story, thumbnail, email header. Describe each layout.
6. A before and after: rewrite one of my samples in the new voice.

Keep it premium and simple. One page.

Write a short video script.

Topic: {{topic}}
Who's watching: {{audience}}
Length: {{length}}
Call to action: {{cta}}
On camera or voiceover: {{format}}

Structure:
1. Hook (first 2 seconds): what I say and what's on screen.
2. Setup (why this matters to them), one line.
3. Three beats: each one line of speech plus the shot or on-screen text.
4. Payoff: the thing they came for.
5. Call to action, one line.

Also give: on-screen captions (short, punchy), the B-roll shot list, and two alternate hooks. Keep sentences short enough to say in one breath. No claims I can't back up.

Write text-to-video prompts for these shots. I'll paste them into {{tool}}.

What the video is for: {{purpose}}
Shots I need: {{shots}}
Look and feel: {{look}}
Aspect ratio and length per clip: {{format}}

For each shot write one prompt with, in this order: subject, action, setting, camera (angle, lens in mm, movement such as slow push-in or handheld), lighting, color and mood, and what to avoid (text, extra fingers, logos, warped faces).
Then write:
- An image prompt for a first frame, if the tool supports image-to-video.
- A short negative prompt.
- Two variations to try if the first result misses.

Keep each prompt under 80 words. Make the shots match each other so they cut together (same light, same palette, same lens family). No real people's likeness, no brand logos I don't own.

Outline a long video.

Topic: {{topic}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Length: {{length}}
What I want viewers to do after: {{cta}}

Give me:
1. Five title options and three thumbnail ideas (text under 4 words each).
2. The first 30 seconds word for word: proof (why listen to me, only true things), promise (what they'll get), plan (what's coming).
3. Chapters with timestamps, each with the point, an example or demo, and a re-hook line to keep people watching.
4. Where to mention my offer (once in the middle, once at the end) and the exact wording.
5. Ten clips I can cut from it for shorts, with timestamps.

Here's my current homepage hero (the first screen people see):

Headline: {{headline}}
Subhead: {{subhead}}
Button: {{button}}
Visual: {{visual}}
Who it's for: {{audience}}

Critique it hard:
1. The 5-second test: what would a stranger think this business does? Is that right?
2. What feels generic, vague or "AI-made", line by line.
3. What's missing: a concrete outcome, who it's for, proof, a reason to act now.

Then give me three new heroes, each a different direction:
A. Outcome-led (the result they get).
B. Demonstration-led (show the product working in the hero).
C. Bold and brand-led (a strong line, premium feel).
For each: headline under 10 words, subhead under 20, button text, the visual described precisely (layout, motion, imagery), and a one-line reason it works.

Rules: no buzzwords (unlock, revolutionize, seamless, supercharge), no claims I can't prove, no invented numbers. Pick the one you'd ship and say why.

Make a homepage mock-up for a business I want to pitch. It's a sample to show them, not their live site.

Business: {{business}}
What their current site gets wrong: {{problems}}
What their customers need to do on the site: {{jobs}}
Their public info I can use (services, hours, area, photos): {{info}}

Build a single HTML file with: a hero that says what they do and where, a clear booking or call button, services with prices if public, proof (their real public reviews only, quoted with the source, or leave a placeholder), service area, hours, and a footer. Mobile first. Fast. Clean.

Put a small banner at the top: "Sample homepage made for {{business}} by {{me}}."

After the code, write a 3-line message to send them with a screenshot, pointing to the two biggest improvements.

Plan my store launch.

What I sell: {{products}}
Platform: {{platform}}
Who buys: {{buyer}}
Launch date: {{date}}
Budget: {{budget}}

Give me:
1. Store structure: pages, collections, navigation.
2. Product page template: title formula, the first three lines of the description, what photos to shoot (list of 6), size or spec details, shipping and returns in plain words.
3. Pricing check: cost, price, margin per item, and the free-shipping threshold that makes sense.
4. Apps or settings to turn on first (reviews, email capture, abandoned cart, analytics), only the essentials.
5. A drop-style launch: 14-day countdown plan with content each day, an early-access list, and launch day steps.
6. The 10 things to check before going live (checkout test, tax, shipping zones, policies, mobile speed).

Rules: no fake scarcity or fake reviews. If I say "limited", it has to be true.

Build a simple 12-month forecast for my business as a table I can paste into a spreadsheet.

What I sell and prices: {{offers}}
Where I am today (customers, monthly revenue, monthly costs): {{today}}
Where my buyers come from and what it costs: {{channels}}
Hours I can work: {{hours}}

Make three scenarios: slow, expected, strong. For each month show: new customers, customers lost, total customers, revenue, costs (fixed and variable), profit, and cash at month end.

Rules:
- List every assumption in its own table (conversion rates, churn, price changes, hours per customer) so I can change them. Label each as "my data" or "your guess".
- Flag the month I run out of hours to deliver, if I do, and what I'd need to hire or automate.
- Show the formulas in plain words so I can rebuild them.
- This is a planning tool, not a promise. Say which two assumptions matter most and how to test them in the next 30 days.

Work out my unit economics.

Price and billing: {{price}}
Cost to deliver one month of service: {{delivery_cost}}
Payment fees: {{fees}}
How long customers stay on average (or my guess): {{retention}}
What I spend on marketing now, and what it brought in: {{acquisition}}

Calculate and explain in plain words:
1. Gross profit per customer in the first 30 days.
2. Gross profit over a customer's lifetime.
3. The most I can spend in marketing per new sale and still break even in 30 days.
4. The most I should ever pay (lifetime profit divided by 3).
5. My current cost per customer, if I gave you the numbers, and whether it's healthy.
6. Three ways to raise first-30-day cash (an upfront fee, an annual option, an add-on) and the effect of each.

Show the math step by step. Mark guesses clearly.

Check my cash and spending.

Cash I have now: {{cash}}
Monthly revenue (average of last 3 months): {{revenue}}
Every recurring cost (name, amount, what it does): {{costs}}
One-off costs coming up: {{upcoming}}

Give me:
1. Monthly burn and runway in months, at today's numbers.
2. Each recurring cost rated keep, cut, or downgrade, with the reason (does it make money, save real time, or neither).
3. Free or cheaper swaps for anything I should downgrade.
4. Runway after the cuts.
5. The revenue I'd need to break even each month.
6. A rule for future spending: what a new tool must do to earn its place.

Use only the numbers I gave you. This is planning help, not financial advice.

Write a quote for this job that's clear enough that the customer says yes without questions.

Customer: {{customer}}
The job: {{job}}
My prices: {{prices}}
Terms I want (deposit, due date, cancellation): {{terms}}
My business details: {{me}}

Include: a short summary of the job in their words, line items with quantities and prices, what's included and not included, timeline, total, deposit and how to pay, how long the quote is valid, and a one-line yes ("Reply YES and I'll send the deposit link").

Then write the matching invoice text for after they say yes, and a polite reminder for 3 days after the due date. Keep both friendly and short.

Plan my day. I have {{hours}} hours for the business today.

My goal this week: {{goal}}
My pipeline (who's where): {{pipeline}}
My to-do list: {{todos}}
Fixed commitments today: {{calendar}}

Give me:
1. The three things that move the goal most. Do these first.
2. A time-blocked schedule for my available hours, with 10-minute buffers.
3. Who to message today and the first line for each (follow-ups due, warm replies, new outreach count).
4. What to drop, delegate or push to tomorrow, and why.
5. One thing to automate or template so I never do it by hand again.

Be ruthless. If my list is too long for my hours, cut it and say what you cut.

Audit my week and build an automation plan.

What my business does: {{business}}
Every recurring task, how often, and minutes each time: {{tasks}}
Tools I use: {{tools}}
Budget for new tools per month: {{budget}}

For each task decide: automate, template, delegate, batch, or drop. Then:
1. Rank by hours saved per month for the effort to set up.
2. For the top 5, give the exact setup: trigger, steps, tools, and what a human still checks. Prefer tools I already have.
3. Which tasks an AI assistant could run on a schedule (a daily or weekly report, a draft queue) with my approval before anything sends.
4. What must never be automated (anything that sends money, signs agreements, or speaks for me without review).
5. Total hours saved per month and total new cost.

Keep the setup steps beginner-friendly.
